Abstract
Electrochemical water-splitting is an ideal strategy to produce the promising substitutable energy source, hydrogen $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ . However, the sluggish kinetics of electrochemical oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and the prohibitive cost, low reserves and easy oxidation of noble metal-based electrocatalysts force researchers to explore efficient and low-cost electrocatalysts. Bimetal nanostructred materials are proved to have enhanced OER catalytic performances. In this study, a series of bimetallic metal-organic frameworks (Fe/Ni-MOFs) are prepared by a solvothermal method. The prepared MOFs present abundant unsaturated metal active sites for OER. The optimized Fe/Ni bimetal-MOF has low overpotentials of $236~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $284~\mathrm{mV}$ at $100~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for OER. In addition, in comparison with most of the previously reported OER electrocatalysts, the present MOF shows a lower Tafel slope of 49 mV dec-1. Besides, the MOF catalyst exhibits high electrochemical stability and the OER activity shows a negligible change after stability test for $15\mathrm{~h~}$ and 10000 voltammetric cycles. Meanwhile, the Fe-doped Ni-MOFs show faster catalytic kinetics and higher conductivity than the monometallic Ni-MOF. This work paves a way to exploit bi- or multi-metallic MOFs with high conductivities and electrocatalytic performances for electrochemical energy conversion.
Keywords: Metal-organic framework, bimetal, oxygen evolution reaction, electrocatalysis, catalyst, water splitting
## 1. Introduction
Fast growing energy consumption, environmental issues and energy crisis have forced human to develop more efficient and sustainable energy conversion and storage systems. Hydrogen has been regarded as the appealing candidate for the next generation energy carrier owing to its high gravimetric energy density, zero contaminant and renewability.[1-4] Electrochemical water-splitting is considered to be the most efficient way for hydrogen production because of its low cost, high purity and environment amity. However, the sluggish kinetics of electrochemical water oxidation arose from its proton-coupled multi-electron transfer makes water-splitting still a key challenge. [5, 6] Up to now, $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ have been widely used as the most efficient oxygen evolution reaction (OER) electrocatalysts in both alkaline and acidic solutions. Unfortunately, their low global reserves, high cost, easy oxidation and dissolution during water splitting process severely hamper their widespread real application.[7, 8] Thus, it’s urgent to exploit efficient alternative catalysts based on inexpensive and earth-abundant elements.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which have high specific surface area, tunable ordered porous structure and abundant intrinsic molecular metal sites, have been regarded as ideal catalyst candidates.[9-11] However, their low conductivities and poor stabilities greatly hinder their wild application as electrochemical catalysts. Therefore, many efforts have been made to increase the conductivities of these materials. To this end, one effective method is to hybridize MOFs with secondary highly conductive supports, like graphene,[12, 13] polyaniline[14, 15] and so on. However, with this method, the micropores of MOFs may be blocked, which can severely limit their effective mass transport during electrocatalysis. In recent years, using MOFs as the sacrifices to prepare high-performance electrocatalysts at high temperatures, such as metal oxides, porous carbon, metal sulfides etc., have attracted intense research interests.[16-19] Nevertheless, high-temperature carbonation process may destroy the ordered channel structures of MOFs and sacrifice the intrinsic metal active sites for catalysis.
Bimetallic OER electrocatalysts, especially Fe/Ni-based electrocatalysts, have been proved to have enhanced OER catalytic performances.[20-24] Corrigan et al. found that the OER activity of NiOOH can be significantly improved by introducing Fe impurity even as little as 1 ppm into the electrolyte and the activity can be further enhanced with increasing the co-precipitated Fe content into nickel oxide thin films.[21] Recently, Shannon W. Boettcher and co-workers also confirmed that $200~\mathrm{mV}$ of overpotential for OER can be lowered when the Fe content in the $\mathrm{Ni_{1-x}F e_{x}O O H}$ catalyst reaches $25\%$ .[25] An acknowledged reason is that the Fe-doping can increase the electrical conductivities of the Ni-based OER catalysts. In recent years, FeNi-bimetal MOFs have also exhibited superior OER catalytic activities and Fe-doped Ni-MOFs usually have higher electrical conductivities.[26, 27] For example, Zhao et al. reported that compared with monometallic Ni-MOF, the synthesized NiFe-MOF array has a higher electrical conductivity of $(1\pm0.2){\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{m}^{-1}$ and showed a lower overpotential for OER at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $240~\mathrm{mV}$ vs $306~\mathrm{mV}$ for Ni-MOF). [27] Besides, theoretical computations and experiments demonstrated that trivalent, tetravalent, 3p or transition metal cations can effectively strengthen the electrostatic interactions between the metal nodes and carboxylic groups, thus improve the stability of MOFs.[20, 28, 29] For instance, Zhou’s group synthesized a kind of very stable bimetal-organic frameworks of $\mathrm{PCN-}250(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co})$ , which can remain powder patterns unaltered without phase transition in water for 6 months and in aqueous solutions with a wide pH range from 1 to 11 for $24\mathrm{h}$ .[28]
Herein, Ni-BTC (BTC: 1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid) MOFs doped with various contents of Fe were prepared and their OER electrocatalytic performances were investigated without high-temperature annealing treatment. BTC MOF with dimetal paddlewheel secondary building units (SBUs) bridged by four carboxylate groups presents coordinatively unsaturated metal centers which can serve as catalytically active sites for OER.[30, 31] Meanwhile, Fe(III)-doping in Ni-BTC MOF can increase not only the electrical conductivity but also the stability of the MOF. So far, some reported FeNi-MOFs grown on nickel foam (NF) by solvothermal or electrochemical methods showed excellent OER catalytic performances.[27, 31, 32] In this work, the prepared bimetal BTC MOF can be directly deposited on NF as electrocatalyst for OER without the usual post-annealing process. As expected, the NF-supported bimetallic Fe/Ni-MOF with appropriate composition shows comparable OER catalytic activity to most of the NF-based OER electrocatalysts.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals and materials
1, 3, 5-trimesic acid $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ , $99\%$ ) and 2-methyl-imidazole (MeIM) were purchased from Aladdin. ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ (AR, $99\%$ ) was obtained from Alfa Aesar. $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was purchased from Xilong Chemical Co., Ltd. $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (AR), ethanol (AR), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, AR) and potassium hydroxide (AR) were obtained from Beijing Chemical Works. All materials were used without further purification. Water used in this work is ultrapure water (Millipore, 18.3 MΩ cm, 25 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .
2.2 Synthesis of FeNi-BTC MOFs.
FeNi-BTC MOFs were prepared following a previously reported procedure.[31, 33] FeNi-BTC MOFs with different $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ratios were prepared as follows. A series of mixtures of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Ni(NO3)2·6H2O (molar ratios of $\mathrm{Fe(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9H_{2}O/N i(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ are 0:1, 1:10, 1:3 and 1:1, respectively, and the total molar amount of $\mathrm{Fe}+\mathrm{Ni}$ is $1.3\mathrm{mmol}$ ) were prepared firstly. Meanwhile, $0.205\ \mathrm{g}$ of 1, 3, 5-trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ and $0.055\mathrm{~g~}$ of MeIM were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The above prepared solutions were transferred into a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and then heated at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days. After cooling to room temperature, the product was washed subsequently with DMF and ethanol for 3 times, and finally dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained FeNi-BTC MOFs with Fe/Ni ratios of 0:1, 1:10, 1:3 and 1:1 are denoted as Ni-BTC, $\mathrm{FeNi}_{10}$ -BTC, ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC and FeNi-BTC, respectively.
For comparison, Ni-BTC MOF without using MeIM was also prepared by the previously reported procedure with a little modification.[34] Typically, 1.8 mmol $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 6 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ were added into $6~\mathrm{ml}$ of mixed solvents (DMF:
$\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{=}1{:}1$ , v/v) to get a homogeneous solution. Then the solution was added into a 20 ml Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $150~\mathrm{{^oC}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was washed with ethanol and dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . To avoid misunderstanding, the prepared catalyst was assigned as Ni-MOF (pure).
## 2.3 Characterizations
An XL30 ESEMFEG scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) operated at an accelerating voltage of $30~\mathrm{\kV}$ and the techniques of energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) were used to characterize the morphologies and compositions of the as-prepared MOF materials. The samples for SEM were prepared by dropping ethanol dispersion of samples onto silicon wafer using pipettes and dried under ambient condition. The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) was measured by a D8 ADVANCE (BRUKER Germany) with Cu-K radiation $(\lambda~=~1.54~\mathrm{~\AA~})$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out on a VG Thermo ESCALAB 250 spectrometer (VG Scientific) operated at 120 W. The compositions of the products were also acquired by using an inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectrometer (ICP-AES, X Series 2，Thermo Scientific USA).
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
All the electrochemical experiments were performed in a standard three-electrode electrochemical cell by a CHI 750D electrochemical workstation under ambient atmosphere using 1 M KOH aqueous solution as electrolyte. The counter electrode and reference electrode are a graphite plate and an $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ with saturated KCl solution, respectively. The catalyst inks were prepared by dissolving $4\:\mathrm{mg}$ of catalysts into the mixture of water and ethanol $(\mathrm{v/v}=3;2,1\ \mathrm{ml})$ , which contains ${5\upmu\mathrm{l}}$ of Nafion $(5\%)$ . The working electrode was prepared by modifying a nickel foam (NF, $5\mathrm{mm}\times$ $5\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) (geometric area $=0.25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) with $30~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ catalyst inks by drop-casting method. All the electrochemical data were corrected with $80\%$ iR drop unless otherwise noted. The measured potentials (vs $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl)}$ are converted against RHE by the equation below:
$$
E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}+0.197+0.059p H
$$
The overpotential of OER was calculated as follows:
$$
\eta=E_{\mathrm{RHE}}-I.23\:\mathrm{V}.
$$
The Tafel slope for OER was calculated by the following equation:
$$
E=a+b*l o g j
$$
where $E$ denotes the potential (vs RHE), $b$ refers to the Tafel slope, $j$ represents the current density.
The accelerated durability tests (ADTs) were performed by chronopotentiometric curves at the potential corresponding to the current density of around $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and by cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests between 1.1 to $1.5\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) under 10,000 cycles at a scan rate of $100\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ in 1 M KOH. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) experiments were performed at $1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ with a frequency range between $10~\mathrm{KHz}$ and $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ and an AC amplitude of $5\mathrm{mV}$ .
The turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated according to the following equation (assuming that every metal atom is involved in the catalysis):
$$
{\mathrm{TOF}}=j\times S/(4\times F\times n)
$$
where $j\left(\mathrm{mA}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right)$ ) is the measured current density at $\eta=300\mathrm{mV}$ , $S$ is the surface area of the electrode, the number 4 means 4 electrons $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution, $F$ is the Faraday constant $(96485.3\mathrm{~C~mol^{-1}})$ ), and $n$ is the mole of metal atoms coated on the electrode.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Preparation and characterizations of BTC MOFs
The Ni-BTC and Fe/Ni-BTC were synthesized through the solvothermal synthesis method reported by Wang and Wade et al.[30, 33, 35], as shown in Scheme 1. The Fe/NiBTC MOFs were prepared by varying the molar ratio of bimetal sources (named as $\mathrm{FeNi_{x}}$ -BTC, $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ is the molar ratio of Ni/Fe). The crystal structures of the MOFs were firstly examined by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD). As displayed in Fig. 1a, the XRD patterns of the as-synthesized MOFs agree well with the simulated pattern derived from single crystal X-ray diffraction, demonstrating the successful formation of BTC-MOF. However, for the bimetallic MOFs, with the increase of Fe content, the diffraction intensities in the XRD patterns decrease, demonstrating the decrease of crystallinity. The compositions of the MOFs and the valence states of the metal elements are studied by XPS. Firstly, the survey XPS results shown in Fig. S1a indicate that bimetallic composites have been obtained. As shown in Fig. 1b, the high resolution XPS of Ni can be deconvoluted into four peaks. The peaks at 855.9 and $873.5~\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , and the peaks located at 861.3 and $880~\mathrm{eV}$ belong to the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ satellite peaks. From the Fe 2p XPS (Fig. 1c), the peaks at 711.8 and $725.4~\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ . Another peak at 718.2 eV is ascribed to the satellite peak. These XPS results confirm that Ni and Fe species in the as-synthesized MOFs are in the $+2$ and $+3$ oxidation states, respectively. To verify the real Fe/Ni ratios in the products of Fe/Ni-BTC MOFs, both energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Fig. S2, Table S1) and inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES; Table S1) were performed. Compared with the Fe/Ni ratios in the precursors, the ratios of Fe/Ni in all the MOF products are actually larger, indicating that Fe element is easier to coordinate with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ . On the other hand, from Table S1, similar molar ratios of Fe/Ni can be obtained from EDS and ICP-AES measurements for all the prepared samples. However, the real mass percentages of metals obtained from EDS are lower than those from the ICP-AES measurements, as a result of the large amount of oxygen atoms in silicon wafer substrate and carbon atoms in carbon conductive adhesive.
The morphologies of the BTC MOFs were then characterized by SEM. The SEM images of different MOFs in Fig. S3 clearly show that the size and shape of the Fe/NiBTC MOFs are strongly dependent on the ratio of Fe/Ni. With increasing the amount of Fe, MOFs with more regular shape can be obtained and the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC MOF shows cubic shape with a uniform size. However, with further increasing the Fe/Ni ratio, for the FeNi-BTC, except for large cubic particles, small irregular debris is also formed. As a result, appropriate Fe content is needed for the formation of regular Fe/Ni-BTC MOF and the FeNi3-BTC has satisfactory size distribution of $3.8~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and morphology (Fig. 2a and Fig. S3c). It should be pointed out that the cube-shaped MOFs and the effect of Fe-doping on the size and morphology of MOFs have also been found in the reported works.[36, 37] In addition, the XPS and energy-dispersive Xray spectroscopy (EDS) measurements reveal that the prepared MOFs also contain N element (Fig. S1 and Fig. S2). From the N 1s XPS (Fig. 1d), the peak at $399.9\mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to pyrrolic-N, which should be from the precursor of 2-methyl-imidazole for the MOF synthesis. Meanwhile, the elemental mapping shown in Fig. 2b clearly demonstrates that the elements of the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC have highly uniform distributions in the formed cubes.
## 3.2 Electrocatalytic performances of the as-prepared Fe/Ni-BTC MOFs toward the OER
The electrocatalytic activities of the synthesized Fe/Ni-BTC MOFs towards OER were first evaluated using a typical three-electrode system in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH solution. Fig. S4 shows the LSVs of OER on the FeNi3-BTC MOF supported on glassy carbon electrode and Ni foam. Clearly, the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/NF shows higher OER catalytic performance than ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/GC. Therefore, in the following studies all the prepared MOF catalysts were deposited on NF by drop-casting method with a fixed mass loading of $0.48\mathrm{~mg~cm^{-2}}$ and used as the working electrode directly. In the electrochemical measurements, the MOF-modified electrodes were first conditioned by successive 20 cyclic voltammetry (CV) scans (at $100\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ ) between $1.0\textrm{-}1.8\textrm{V}$ vs. RHE to ensure a stable electrochemical performance. Then, their OER catalytic activities were examined by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ - saturated 1 M KOH solution at the rotation rate of $1600~\mathrm{rmp}$ and the scan rate of 10 $\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ . For comparison, the OER catalytic performance of ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ was also examined and an $80\%$ iR correction was applied to all original data.
Fig. 3a displays the LSV curves of various catalysts in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH. As observed in Fig. 3a and Fig. S4, there is an oxidation current peak around $1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ , which can be assigned to the formation of $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ . According to the LSV curves of OER (Fig. 3a), all the prepared bimetallic MOF electrocatalysts display more negative potentials and higher current densities in comparison with the monometallic Ni-BTC MOF. And meanwhile, the bimetallic MOF also have smaller Tafel slopes (Fig. 3b), suggesting their more efficient kinetics for OER. Note that, among all the bimetallic FeNi-BTC MOF catalysts, the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC exhibits the highest OER catalytic activity. Here, to quantitatively compare the electrocatalytic activities of the studied MOFs, their overpotentials for OER at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $\left(\eta_{10}\right)$ were recorded. As presented in Fig. 3a and Fig. 3c, the FeNi3-BTC displays the smallest overpotential value of $236~\mathrm{mV}$ , which is lower than those of Ni-BTC $(296~\mathrm{mV})$ , $\mathrm{FeNi}_{10}$ -BTC (277 mV), FeNi-BTC $(257~\mathrm{mV})$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $329~\mathrm{mV},$ ). It should be noted that the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}.$ - BTC is also one of the best OER electrocatalysts among the reported MOF-based electrocatalysts in alkaline electrolyte with small overpotential (Table S2). Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 3b, the Tafel slope of ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC derived from the LSV curve is $49\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ , which is very close to those of the well-studied mixed Fe/Ni catalysts and smaller than those of ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ (56 mV dec-1), Ni-BTC (71 mV dec-1), $\mathrm{FeNi}_{10}$ -BTC $(60\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ ) and FeNi-BTC $(50\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1}})$ , demonstrating its more favorable catalytic kinetics for OER. On the other hand, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements further confirm such result, as shown in
Fig. 3d. At the same potential of $1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ , the $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ -BTC exhibits the smallest semicircular diameter, indicating the highest electrical conductivity and the fastest catalytic kinetics among the studied BTC-MOFs. Besides, all the prepared bimetallic FeNi-BTC MOFs have lower electron transfer resistance than the monometallic NiBTC MOF at 1.5 V, demonstrating that Fe-doping in Ni-MOFs is beneficial to increase their electrical conductivity and thus enhance their OER catalytic activities.
To reveal the contribution of MeIM to the OER catalytic activity of the BTC MOFs, we also synthesized Ni-BTC MOF without the addition of MeIM, assigned as NiBTC (pure), following a previously reported procedure with a little modification.[34]. Meanwhile, to avoid the effect of Ni foam, the OER performance of the Ni-BTC (pure) was measured by depositing on glassy carbon electrode without iR correction. As observed from Fig. S5, both Ni-BTC and Ni-BTC (pure) show almost same OER catalytic activity. As a result, the contribution of MeIM to the OER performance can be ignored and the catalytic activity should be from the prepared MOFs.
The turnover frequencies (TOFs) were also employed to evaluate the intrinsic OER activities of the prepared MOF catalysts. Here, both Fe and Ni in BTC MOFs are regarded as active sites for OER. With an overpotential of $300~\mathrm{mV}$ applied, the TOF values of $\mathrm{FeNi}_{10}$ -BTC $(0.032\ \mathrm{s^{-1}})$ , ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC $(0.280~\mathrm{s^{-1}})$ and FeNi-BTC $(0.080\ \mathrm{s^{-1}})$ were obtained and they are apparently higher than that of Ni-BTC $(0.019\ \mathrm{s^{-1}})$ , further confirming the excellent OER performances of the bimetallic catalysts. The plots of TOF vs. potential for the synthesized BTC-MOF catalysts are shown in Fig. S6. Obviously, the TOF values of ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC are the highest among all the obtained catalysts, demonstrating again its superior OER catalytic activity.
The long-term stability is another crucial criterion to evaluate an electrocatalyst for real application. Here, the stabilities of the prepared MOFs are assessed with chronopotentiometric curves and CV cycling tests. As shown in Fig. 4a, in the chronopotentiometric curve of ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/NF, the current increases first and then decreases slowly with a negligible loss, suggesting the high durability of ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC. Moreover, as shown in Fig. 4a inset, the LSV curve even has a negative shift after 15 h of chronopotentiometric test, suggesting the lowering of overpotential of OER. The improved OER catalytic activity and stability could be ascribed to the NF support. Meanwhile, Fig. 4b compares the LSV curves of the $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ -BTC/NF before and after 10,000 potential cycles. Obviously, there is a nearly $35~\mathrm{mV}$ negative shift of the potential after 10,000 cycles, also confirming the excellent catalytic activity and stability of the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/NF. To further investigate the effect of the NF, we also measured the stabilities of blank NF electrode and ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/GCE. As shown in Fig. 4c, the LSV curve of the blank NF electrode also has a $37~\mathrm{mV}$ negative shift after 10,000 potential cycles, consistent with the stability test of FeNi3-BTC/NF. However, if FeNi3-BTC is deposited on GCE, the LSV curve from ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC/GCE shows a little positive shift after 10,000 cycles, suggesting the degradation of the catalytic activity. These results suggest that using Ni foam can significantly enhance the activity and durability of $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ -BTC. In the present work, the high OER catalytic performance of the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC MOF could be attributed to the large surface area, highly porous structure, abundant metal sites, high electric conductivity and the
appropriate Fe content in the MOF.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we designed and solvothermally synthesized a series of bimetallic MOFs with various Fe/Ni ratios. Due to the high electronic conductivity, the assynthesized FeNi-BTC MOFs supported on Ni foam can be directly employed as electrocatalysts for OER without post-annealing treatment. The SEM measurements indicated that the morphology and size of the BTC-MOF are strongly affected by the added Fe/Ni ratio, and the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC has a regular cubic shape with uniform particle size. Notably, all the synthesized bimetallic MOFs showed higher OER catalytic activity compared with monometallic MOFs. Among the obtained MOFs, the ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ - BTC exhibited the highest OER performance with an overpotential of $236~\mathrm{mV}$ at 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a small Tafel slop of 49 mV dec-1. Moreover, the OER catalytic activity of FeNi3-BTC is higher than most of the previously reported MOF-based OER electrocatalysts. Meanwhile, the bimetal ${\mathrm{FeNi}}_{3}$ -BTC MOF has high electrocatalytic stability and the OER activity remains unchanged over 15 h of continuous electrochemical test. These electrochemical results show that the excellent electrocatalytic activities and stabilities of the MOFs synthesized in this work make them have potential real applications in water splitting. This work may pave a way to exploit novel conductive MOFs as promising electrochemical energy catalysts after optimizing the structure and composition.
